E=[
(2,1904,"Mysteries","Darkness and panic at Memphis","At 10 a.m. intense darkness covers Memphis, Tennessee for about fifteen minutes. In some quarters people shout and pray, imagining the end of the world has come. The Monthly Weather Review blamed a very dense cloud.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Monthly Weather Review, 32-522","intense darkness at Memphis, Tenn., for about fifteen minutes, at 10 A.M., Dec. 2, 1904"),
(6,1893,"Falls","A four-pound lump of ice in Texas","A lump of ice weighing four pounds falls in Texas. Fort cites Scientific American in a list of large ice falls that he reads as pieces of a frozen sky.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Scientific American, 68-58","lump of ice weighing four pounds, Texas, Dec. 6, 1893"),
(7,1872,"Sky","A haycock hurtles over Banbury","People near King's Sutton, Banbury see something like a haycock hurtling through the air, accompanied by fire and dense smoke, with a noise like a railway train. Nature quoted the Birmingham Morning News.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Nature, 7-112","saw, about one o'clock, Dec. 7, 1872, something like a haycock hurtling through the air"),
(9,1891,"Sky","The Lyons light first appears","On Dec. 9 a bright light first appears in the sky above Lyons, N.Y. It returns every 27th night until April 23, 1892. Dr. M. A. Veeder calls it auroral and Fort suspects signalling.","NL","New Lands, citing Scientific American, May 7, 1892","Lyons, N. Y., Dec. 9, 1891"),
(10,1881,"Mysteries","Walter Powell vanishes with a balloon","Walter Powell and two companions ascend from Bath in the Government balloon Saladin. It comes down at Bridport on the coast, but Powell, who had stayed aboard after the others dropped off, is carried away and never found.","NL","New Lands, citing Valentine and Tomlinson, Travels in Space, p. 227","Upon December 10, 1881, Walter Powell and two companions ascended from Bath in the Government balloon"),
(11,1854,"Falls","Ice flakes weighing pounds at Poorhundur","At Poorhundur, India, flat pieces of ice, many weighing several pounds, fall from the sky and are described as 'large ice-flakes'. Fort uses them as evidence of vast ice fields beyond the atmosphere.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Rept. Brit. Assoc., 1855-37","at Poorhundur, India, Dec. 11, 1854, flat pieces of ice"),
(13,1887,"Falls","A bloodlike fall in Cochin China","A substance like blood, somewhat coagulated, falls in Cochin China. L'Annee Scientifique reported it, and Fort compares a thick, viscous red matter that fell at Ulm in 1812.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing L'Annee Scientifique, 1888-75","Dec. 13, 1887, there fell, in Cochin China, a substance like blood"),
(14,1830,"Falls","Black insects fall with snow at Orenburg","A multitude of small black insects fall with snow at Orenburg, Russia. They are said to be gnats, but also said to have flea-like motions. The American Journal of Science reported it.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Amer. Jour. Sci., 1-22-375","Dec. 14, 1830, of a multitude of small, black insects"),
(15,1909,"Sky","Crowds in Rome watch Venus at noon","Crowds in the streets of Rome watch Venus at noon. Fort quotes the New York Sun and links the planet's brightness to the wave of mysterious airships reported across New England that month.","NL","New Lands, citing New York Sun, Dec. 16, 1909","upon the 15th of December, 1909, crowds stood, at noon, in the streets of Rome, watching it"),
(17,1896,"Sky","An explosion in the sky shakes Britain","At 5:30 a.m. an explosion in the sky accompanies the severest earthquake felt in the British Isles in the nineteenth century, apart from the Colchester shock. Fort lists reports of lights and booms from across the country.","NL","New Lands, citing Observations upon the Earthquake of Dec. 17, 1896","5.30 A. M., Dec. 17, 1896, by an explosion in the sky"),
(19,1919,"Cosmos","A black-ink mark near Littrow","J. W. Scholes of Huddersfield sees a 'very conspicuous black-ink mark' near the lunar crater Littrow. W. J. West of Gosport reported having seen it on the 7th.","NL","New Lands, citing English Mechanic, 110-257","upon Dec. 19, 1919, he saw, near the lunar crater Littrow"),
(20,1893,"Sky","A luminous body crosses the Carolinas","On the morning of Dec. 20 many people in Virginia, North Carolina and South Carolina see a luminous body pass overhead from west to east. L'Astronomie reported it.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing L'Astronomie, 1894-157","upon the morning of Dec. 20, 1893, an appearance in the sky was seen by many persons in Virginia, North Carolina, and South Carolina"),
(21,1882,"Sky","An unknown body beside the Sun at Broughty Ferry","Between 10 and 11 a.m. at Broughty Ferry, Scotland, a correspondent sees an unknown luminous body near and a little above the Sun. Another letter claims it was Venus and 'no other'. Dundee Advertiser.","NL","New Lands, citing Dundee Advertiser, Dec. 22 & 25, 1882","at Broughty Ferry, Scotland, a correspondent had seen an unknown luminous body"),
(22,1835,"Cosmos","A star-like light in Aristarchus","Francis Bailey reports a star-like light in the lunar crater Aristarchus. Fort lists it among the 'carnivals upon the moon'.","NL","New Lands, citing Proctor, Myths and Marvels, p. 329","Dec. 22, 1835-the star-like light in Aristarchus"),
(24,1909,"Sky","New Yorkers stare at Venus","At 3 p.m. crowds stand in the streets of New York watching Venus, reported by the New York Tribune. Fort places the crowds in a month of 'mysterious airship' reports.","NL","New Lands, citing New York Tribune, Dec. 25, 1909","afternoon of December 24th crowds stood in the streets of New York, watching Venus"),
(28,1860,"Falls","Red rain at Siena","At about 7 a.m. in northwestern Siena, a reddish rain falls copiously for two hours, a second shower at 11, and again three days later. Prof. Campini wrote to Prof. Matteucci.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Year Book of Facts, 1861-273","upon Dec. 28, 1860, at about 7 A.M., in the northwestern part of Siena, a reddish rain fell copiously for two hours"),
(30,1850,"Haunts","Phantom soldiers near the Bann","Phantom soldiers are seen in the sky near the Bann mouth. Fort cites the British Association report with similar sky armies at Buderich in 1854 and at Mohan in 1858.","NL","New Lands, citing Rept. B. A., 1852-30","near the Banmouth, Dec. 30, 1850"),
(31,1909,"Sky","Three huge lights over Huntington","Three huge lights of nearly uniform size appear in the early morning sky near Huntington, West Virginia. Farmer Joseph Green said they were meteors that fell on his farm. Reported in the New York Tribune.","NL","New Lands, citing New York Tribune, Jan. 1, 1910","Three huge lights of almost uniform dimensions appeared in the early morning sky"),
]
